{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>These are the journeys</b> — After the vengeance against Midian, when HaShem said to Moses, \"Afterward you shall be gathered to your people\" (Numbers 31:2), and after he apportioned the land of Sihon and Og and they built the cities mentioned, he set his mind to write the journeys. His intention in this was to make known the kindnesses of HaShem toward them: that even though He decreed upon them to be tossed about and moved in the wilderness, do not suppose that they were continually moving and being tossed from place to place and had no rest; rather, throughout the entire long period they made only forty-two journeys, as the Rav, Rashi, wrote in the name of Rabbi Moshe ha-Darshan. And the Rav added in the Guide for the Perplexed (III:50) a benefit in knowing them, saying that the need to mention the journeys is very great, for the miracles and signs that were performed were true realities for all who saw them, but in the future they will be matters of hearsay and the listener will deny them. And among the signs of the Torah and its mighty wonders are Israel's survival in the wilderness for forty years and the finding of the manna every day — and they are places far from civilization, unnatural for human beings, not a place of seed or fig or grapevine or pomegranate. And the Torah says: \"Bread you did not eat, and wine and strong drink you did not drink\" (Deuteronomy 29:5) — and all these signs are visibly apparent as miraculous acts. And when the Creator, blessed be He, knew that these signs would undergo what the passage of time brings — listeners would not believe in them and would suppose that their survival in this wilderness was near civilization, a place where human beings live, like the deserts where the children of Arabia dwell today, or places where there would be plowing and harvest, or where there are grasses and plants for human food, and that there would be water wells in those places — therefore He removed these thoughts from the hearts of men and reinforced all these signs through the record of the journeys, so that future generations may see them and know the great signs, how human beings survived in those places for forty years. All these are his words. Now, the writing of the journeys is a commandment of HaShem, whether for the reasons mentioned or apart from them; the matter — its secret has not been revealed to us. For \"by the mouth of YHWH\" attaches to \"and Moses wrote,\" not as Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] said, that it attaches to \"for their journeys\" — for He has already informed us of this: \"by the mouth of YHWH they encamped, and by the mouth of YHWH they journeyed\" (Numbers 9:20)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {0 "Ramban cites Maimonides, Guide for the Perplexed III:50 (written in Judeo-Arabic); the reference 'ג נ' corresponds to part III, chapter 50."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:10:16.322335054Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}